WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:24.705
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm David Baumert,

00:00:24.706 --> 00:00:27.030
and I'm a member of

00:00:27.031 --> 00:00:29.745
the Microsoft Strategic
Prototyping team.

00:00:29.746 --> 00:00:32.130
We're in part of the API
and Research Division.

00:00:32.131 --> 00:00:35.950
Today, we brought some
receptionist assistant robots.

00:00:35.951 --> 00:00:38.990
We plan to deploy these
later on in building

00:00:38.991 --> 00:00:43.200
99 to help receptionists be
able to stay at their post,

00:00:43.201 --> 00:00:45.140
yet still do more
within the building,

00:00:45.141 --> 00:00:46.310
and then also be able to

00:00:46.311 --> 00:00:48.035
interact with
a wider range of people.

00:00:48.036 --> 00:00:50.510
This particular robot is going to

00:00:50.511 --> 00:00:53.275
help us retrieve items
from inside the building,

00:00:53.276 --> 00:00:55.140
and then deliver them to people.

00:00:55.141 --> 00:00:59.535
It's using a combination
of local processing,

00:00:59.536 --> 00:01:02.030
and cloud processing to do

00:01:02.031 --> 00:01:04.955
image recognition
and motion planning.

00:01:04.956 --> 00:01:08.660
The result here, is that
very different from

00:01:08.661 --> 00:01:11.990
an industrial setting where
everything is repeated.

00:01:11.991 --> 00:01:14.420
This robot is able to figure out

00:01:14.421 --> 00:01:18.010
the situation at the time,

00:01:18.011 --> 00:01:21.910
and is actually able to

00:01:21.911 --> 00:01:22.990
retrieve the beverage from

00:01:22.991 --> 00:01:27.620
the inside of the refrigerator.

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:41.760
So over here, there's a bit
of review of what's going on.

00:01:41.761 --> 00:01:44.065
Part of the process
for figuring out

00:01:44.066 --> 00:01:47.295
how to move is to
run simulations.

00:01:47.296 --> 00:01:49.900
When a simulation is found

00:01:49.901 --> 00:01:53.155
that actually works
as a solution here,

00:01:53.156 --> 00:01:56.140
then that data is provided to

00:01:56.141 --> 00:01:58.370
the robot that commands
the joints into

00:01:58.371 --> 00:02:00.260
the specific sequences needed

00:02:00.261 --> 00:02:03.870
to actually grab the beverage.

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:17.550
So, one thing we can see
here as we're waiting,

00:02:17.551 --> 00:02:19.550
some of the computing costs of

00:02:19.551 --> 00:02:25.625
these various tasks can

00:02:25.626 --> 00:02:31.080
effect the quality of
this scenario in different ways.

00:02:31.081 --> 00:02:33.385
So, what we're very
interested to know is,

00:02:33.386 --> 00:02:35.690
what makes sense to run
here locally and what

00:02:35.691 --> 00:02:39.030
makes sense to actually
ship up to the cloud?

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:58.415
So, if those kinds
of optimizations of

00:02:58.416 --> 00:03:01.315
where the code is
actually running,

00:03:01.316 --> 00:03:03.255
potentially can speed
up this scenario.

00:03:03.256 --> 00:03:04.340
But as you can see

00:03:04.341 --> 00:03:08.700
it's able to effectively
get the task completed.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:12.620
So, we think this is one step

00:03:12.621 --> 00:03:15.110
along a story that's going to

00:03:15.111 --> 00:03:18.035
continue about
a beautiful relationship

00:03:18.036 --> 00:03:20.490
between humans and robots.

